scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:909:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1992-3-1,commonlii-LKSC-1992-3,(1992) 2 Sri LR 180,LKSC,1992,A matter that has not been raised before may still be a ground for consideration.,"(1) A matter that has not been raised before might, nevertheless, be a ground of",SRC030-s75,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:918:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-1,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,The amalgamation of a lot with another land is possible.,"(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:919:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-2,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,The causes of separate defendants must be considered separately.,"(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:920:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-3,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,"Exclusive, undisturbed, adverse, and uninterrupted possession of a property for a significant period can prove a claim to have prescribed to that property.","(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:921:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1994-18-1,commonlii-LKSC-1994-18,(2002) 3 Sri LR 299,LKSC,1994,A court's discretion to order security for costs under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason.,"(1) The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable. (2) Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend. (3) In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint. (4) In the instant case order for costs was unnecessary and premature in that firstly, the trial could well be concluded within a short time; secondly, at 300 the stage of the",SRC030-s416,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
