scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:909:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1992-3-1,commonlii-LKSC-1992-3,(1992) 2 Sri LR 180,LKSC,1992,"A matter that has not been raised before might, nevertheless, be a ground of","(1) A matter that has not been raised before might, nevertheless, be a ground of",SRC030-s75,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:918:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-1,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,"Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded","(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:919:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-2,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,"The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot","(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:920:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-3,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,"The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5","(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:921:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1994-18-1,commonlii-LKSC-1994-18,(2002) 3 Sri LR 299,LKSC,1994,The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable,"(1) The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable. (2) Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend. (3) In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint. (4) In the instant case order for costs was unnecessary and premature in that firstly, the trial could well be concluded within a short time; secondly, at 300 the stage of the",SRC030-s416,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:922:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1994-18-2,commonlii-LKSC-1994-18,(2002) 3 Sri LR 299,LKSC,1994,"Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend","(1) The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable. (2) Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend. (3) In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint. (4) In the instant case order for costs was unnecessary and premature in that firstly, the trial could well be concluded within a short time; secondly, at 300 the stage of the",SRC030-s416,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:923:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1994-18-3,commonlii-LKSC-1994-18,(2002) 3 Sri LR 299,LKSC,1994,In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint,"(1) The discretion of the court to order security for costs of the defendant under section 416 of the Civil Procedure Code should be exercised judicially and with reason; and the amount ordered should itself be reasonable. (2) Section 416 provides for ""security"" which is required to be furnished, in order to create a fund from which an order for costs made by the court could be satisfied, if such costs are not directly paid by the plaintiff. ""Incurred"" costs under the section means that amount of costs which the court may financially award, regardless of what the party may actually spend. (3) In making the order the court should not be unduly influenced by the view that the plaintiff did not have a prirna facie case or the absence of a valuation report annexed to the plaint. (4) In the instant case order for costs was unnecessary and premature in that firstly, the trial could well be concluded within a short time; secondly, at 300 the stage of the",SRC030-s416,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:941:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1996-16-1,commonlii-LKSC-1996-16,(1997) 1 Sri LR 103,LKSC,1996,"The pleadings in the case disclosed a ""conflict of claims"" between the widow and the executor within the meaning of Section 523 of the Civil Procedure Code, in view of which the claim of the executor has to be preferred to that of the widow. The expression ""conflict of claims"" cannot be confined to a situation where there are opposing","(1) The pleadings in the case disclosed a ""conflict of claims"" between the widow and the executor within the meaning of Section 523 of the Civil Procedure Code, in view of which the claim of the executor has to be preferred to that of the widow. The expression ""conflict of claims"" cannot be confined to a situation where there are opposing",SRC030-s523,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:942:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1996-34-1,commonlii-LKSC-1996-34,(1997) 1 Sri LR 208,LKSC,1996,"Upon the death of an applicant for the acquisition of a land under section 71 of the Finance Act No. 11 of 1963 as amended, there can be substitution of a ""Specified heir"" viz. a person mentioned in Section 71","Upon the death of an applicant for the acquisition of a land under section 71 of the Finance Act No. 11 of 1963 as amended, there can be substitution of a ""Specified heir"" viz. a person mentioned in Section 71(2)(a) - in the prescribed order of priority - as well as of a testate heir. Whether the",SRC028-s36,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:943:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1996-34-2,commonlii-LKSC-1996-34,(1997) 1 Sri LR 208,LKSC,1996,(a) - in the prescribed order of priority - as well as of a testate heir. Whether the,"Upon the death of an applicant for the acquisition of a land under section 71 of the Finance Act No. 11 of 1963 as amended, there can be substitution of a ""Specified heir"" viz. a person mentioned in Section 71(2)(a) - in the prescribed order of priority - as well as of a testate heir. Whether the",SRC028-s36,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:962:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1998-3-1,commonlii-LKSC-1998-3,(1998) 2 Sri LR 105,LKSC,1998,"1. Accordingly, in terms of section 2","1. Accordingly, in terms of section 2 (1) (a) a defendant is entitled to counterclaim for damages for wrongful arrest of a vessel where there was either mala fides or gross negligence which implies malice. Per Fernando, J. "". . . . the power of the court, consistently recognised and exercised over a long period of time to award damages for malicious arrest, is ancillary or incidental to its power to arrest a vessel, and can be exercised in the same proceedings. Such a claim for damages can be made without awaiting the termination of the proceedings."" 2. The High Court has the power to order the plaintiff to give security for the defendant's counter-claim for damages for malicious arrest. 106",SRC030-s654,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:963:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1998-3-2,commonlii-LKSC-1998-3,(1998) 2 Sri LR 105,LKSC,1998,"(a) a defendant is entitled to counterclaim for damages for wrongful arrest of a vessel where there was either mala fides or gross negligence which implies malice. Per Fernando, J. "". . . . the power of the court, consistently recognised and exercised over a long period of time to award damages for malicious arrest, is ancillary or incidental to its power to arrest a vessel, and can be exercised in the same proceedings. Such a claim for damages can be made without awaiting the termination of the proceedings."" 2. The High Court has the power to order the plaintiff to give security for the defendant's counter-claim for damages for malicious arrest. 106","1. Accordingly, in terms of section 2 (1) (a) a defendant is entitled to counterclaim for damages for wrongful arrest of a vessel where there was either mala fides or gross negligence which implies malice. Per Fernando, J. "". . . . the power of the court, consistently recognised and exercised over a long period of time to award damages for malicious arrest, is ancillary or incidental to its power to arrest a vessel, and can be exercised in the same proceedings. Such a claim for damages can be made without awaiting the termination of the proceedings."" 2. The High Court has the power to order the plaintiff to give security for the defendant's counter-claim for damages for malicious arrest. 106",SRC030-s654,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1032:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-2004-21-1,commonlii-LKSC-2004-21,(2005) 1 Sri LR 31,LKSC,2004,1. The defendant failed to establish prescriptive title to the land as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance. 2. The Court of,1. The defendant failed to establish prescriptive title to the land as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance. 2. The Court of,SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:1067:draftly-rule-courts-coa-1095-99-f.pdf-1,courts-coa-1095-99-f.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proving prescriptive title falls on the defendant when the legal title is in the plaintiff.,"It is settled law that where the legal title is in the plaintiff the burden of proving prescriptive title falls on the defendant (Siyanaris Vs Jayasinghe Udeni De Silva 52 NLR 289, Juliana Hamine Vs. Don Thomas 59 NLR 546, Sirajudeen and two others Vs. Abbas 1994 (2) SLR 365).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
